+\subsection{Initialization, Offspring, and Environmental Selection}\label{initialization-offspring-and-environmental-selection}
 
-The kernel is a binary NSGA-II with uniform crossover and bit-flip mutation at rate \(1/n\). Constraint dominance ranks feasible solutions above infeasible ones and compares infeasible solutions by violation. Crowding distance truncates the population to 40 over 40 generations. Keeping this standard kernel isolates the proposed contribution in the variation stage.
+For each of the 40 initial individuals, the implementation draws a selection density \(p_i\sim U(0.03,0.15)\), then samples each bit independently as Bernoulli(\(p_i\)); enabled repair is applied immediately and logged at generation 0. Each generation creates 40 children. Two parent indices are sampled independently with replacement for each child, a per-bit Bernoulli(0.5) mask performs uniform crossover, and each resulting bit flips independently with probability \(1/n\). The local-search bounds in the formal definition are then frozen. Repair is applied to every child, and local search is applied deterministically to child-array indices 0--19, i.e., the first 20 generated children rather than a second random sample.
+
+Environmental selection pools the 40 parents and 40 processed children. Constraint dominance ranks feasible solutions above infeasible ones and compares infeasible solutions by the normalized violation \(v_B\). Non-dominated fronts are admitted in order, and crowding distance truncates the last front to 40 survivors. Candidate indices inherit the deterministic source-pool order. Exact score ties in insertion, substitution removal, and repair are resolved by the lowest candidate index because the implementation takes the first \texttt{argmax}/\texttt{argmin}; no stochastic scientific preference is attached to this rule. Exact crowding-distance ties follow the array order returned by the numerical sorting routine and have no additional project-level tie criterion.
 
 \subsection{Forward Insertion}\label{forward-insertion}
 
-After variation, half of the offspring undergo a forward pass of depth at most eight. While slack remains, the affordable unselected project with the highest equal-weight benefit-to-cost ratio is tentatively inserted. The insertion is retained only if it improves normalized scalarized fitness, defined as the sum of normalized objectives plus a violation penalty. The pass stops at the first rejection. This move converts budget slack left by recombination into a candidate improvement.
+After variation and repair, child indices 0--19 undergo a forward pass of depth at most eight. While slack remains, the affordable unselected project with the highest raw \(\beta_j/\max(c_j,1)\) score after the optional group multiplier is tentatively inserted. The insertion is retained only if it strictly lowers \(\Phi_g\); exact equality is rejection. The pass stops at the first rejection. This move converts budget slack left by recombination into a candidate improvement.
 
 \subsection{Atomic Backward--Forward Substitution}\label{atomic-backwardforward-substitution}
 
-The substitution pass has depth at most four. It removes the selected project with the lowest benefit-to-cost ratio only provisionally, uses the released budget to choose the highest-scoring affordable replacement, and evaluates the resulting portfolio once. Acceptance commits both edits; rejection restores both. The pass stops at the first rejection and never proposes from a portfolio with fewer than two retained projects. This construction records replacement as one paired event rather than as unrelated insert and delete events. Section 6.3 shows that the paired representation does not yield a significant hypervolume change, and its usefulness to reviewers is not evaluated.
+The substitution pass has depth at most four. It provisionally removes the selected project with the lowest raw \(\beta_j/\max(c_j,1)\) ratio, then uses the released budget to choose the highest-scoring affordable replacement under the same optional group multiplier. The removed project is excluded from immediate reinsertion. The resulting portfolio is evaluated once. Acceptance requires a strict decrease in \(\Phi_g\) and commits both edits; equality or worsening restores both. The pass stops at the first rejection and is attempted only when the pre-removal portfolio contains at least three projects. This construction records replacement as one paired event rather than as unrelated insert and delete events. Section 6.3 shows that the paired representation does not yield a significant hypervolume change, and its usefulness to reviewers is not evaluated.
 
