+\subsection{4.1. Initialization, Offspring, and Environmental
+Selection}\label{initialization-offspring-and-environmental-selection}
+
+For each of the 40 initial individuals, the implementation draws a
+selection density \(p_i\sim U(0.03,0.15)\), then samples each bit
+independently as Bernoulli(\(p_i\)); enabled repair is applied immediately
+and logged at generation 0. Each generation creates 40 children. Two
+parent indices are sampled independently with replacement for each child,
+a per-bit Bernoulli(0.5) mask performs uniform crossover, and each
+resulting bit flips independently with probability \(1/n\). The
+local-search bounds in the formal definition are then frozen. Repair is
+applied to every child, and local search is applied deterministically to
+child-array indices 0--19, i.e., the first 20 generated children rather
+than a second random sample.
+
+Environmental selection pools the 40 parents and 40 processed children.
+Constraint dominance ranks feasible solutions above infeasible ones and
+compares infeasible solutions by the normalized violation \(v_B\).
+Non-dominated fronts are admitted in order, and crowding distance
+truncates the last front to 40 survivors. Candidate indices inherit the
+deterministic source-pool order. Exact score ties in insertion,
+substitution removal, and repair are resolved by the lowest candidate
+index because the implementation takes the first \texttt{argmax}/
+\texttt{argmin}; no stochastic scientific preference is attached to this
+rule. Exact crowding-distance ties follow the array order returned by the
+numerical sorting routine and have no additional project-level tie
+criterion.
 
